e('TLS protects a message between your phone and the server. The server then decrypts it, stores it, and re-encrypts it to the recipient. Anyone who controls the server (an insider, an attacker, a government with a warrant) can read everything.','In a messaging service that uses only hop-by-hop TLS, the server decrypts the application messages and can read them before forwarding them over another TLS connection. TLS can also carry already end-to-end encrypted content; in that case terminating TLS does not reveal the inner message.')
e('The Signal protocol is the dominant design: it runs Signal itself, WhatsApp (for all users since 2016), and default encryption in Facebook Messenger since 2023.','The Signal protocol family is used in Signal and other messaging products. Product deployment scope and defaults can change; this lesson explains the protocol rather than claiming every feature or conversation in another product uses the same design.')
e('| Post-compromise security | Future messages become safe again |','| Post-compromise security | Future messages can recover after fresh unknown secrets enter the ratchet and attacker access ends |')
e('| Deniability | Transcript doesn\'t prove authorship |','| Deniability | Shared-key tags alone are not transferable proof of authorship |')
e('his long-term **identity key**.','his long-term **identity public key**; private keys stay on endpoints.')
e('so only the real Alice and the real Bob can compute them. That is **mutual authentication**, without either side signing the message.','binding the exchange to those identity keys. This provides **mutual authentication of keys**; establishing that those keys belong to the intended people requires authenticated key comparison or another trusted binding.')
e('**The one-time prekey** stops replays of Alice\'s first message and protects against a later compromise of SPK_B.','**The one-time prekey**, with its private half deleted after successful use, prevents reusing that prekey to accept the initial exchange again and strengthens protection against later compromise of SPK_B.')
e('Since 2023 Signal uses **PQXDH**: Bob\'s bundle also contains a signed post-quantum prekey (CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024, now standardised as ML-KEM).','Signal announced **PQXDH** in 2023: Bob\'s bundle adds signed post-quantum prekeys. The published specification uses a parameterised KEM and gives CRYSTALS-Kyber-1024 as an example. NIST\'s later ML-KEM standard derives from Kyber, but draft Kyber encodings and the final standard must not be assumed interchangeable.')
e('an attacker who records traffic today must break both X25519 and the KEM.','the hybrid is designed to retain secrecy if either component remains secure under the protocol\'s assumptions.')
e('Whenever a party receives a new one from the other side, it generates its own new ratchet key pair and does a fresh DH between them.','On receiving a new peer ratchet key, it first combines that key with its existing private ratchet key to derive the receiving chain, then generates a new local key pair and combines it with the peer key to derive the sending chain.')
e('After one round trip the keys are out of reach again.','Security recovers once fresh DH contributions unknown to the attacker are mixed in and delivered. The exact recovery point depends on whose state was stolen and which messages arrive; it is not an unconditional one-round-trip promise.')
e('a DH ratchet each time the conversation changes direction','a DH ratchet on receipt of a new peer ratchet public key')
e('so that post-compromise security also holds against a future quantum attacker.','to add post-quantum forward secrecy and post-compromise recovery under its assumptions. Recovery depends on successful delivery of the needed ratchet contributions.')
e('Messages are authenticated with MACs from shared keys, not signatures. Bob knows Alice wrote a message, because only the two of them hold the key. But Bob could have produced that MAC himself, so a transcript does not prove to a third party what Alice said. This is the MAC-versus-signature distinction from the first lesson, chosen deliberately. (It is a cryptographic property; courts have accepted screenshots anyway.)','For the pairwise shared-key authentication illustrated here, a valid tag authenticates the message to a peer who knows they did not create it, assuming uncompromised keys. Either holder could compute the tag, so it alone is not transferable proof of authorship. This is narrower than a claim about all messaging transcripts, group-signature schemes or legal evidence.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> Legal conclusions about screenshots or deniability are omitted because no jurisdiction-specific legal review was performed.')
e('since almost nobody compares fingerprints, WhatsApp (2023) and Apple\'s iMessage Contact Key Verification publish keys in auditable append-only logs, the same idea as Certificate Transparency, so a server cannot quietly show different keys to different people.','auditable key directories can expose inconsistent key views when clients verify proofs and the design\'s monitoring/witness assumptions hold. WhatsApp described its deployment in 2023. Transparency does not by itself prove the real-world owner of a first-seen key or make all substitutions impossible.')
e('**Multiple devices**: each of your devices has its own identity key and its own sessions. Sending to Bob means encrypting once per device he owns ("client-side fan-out"), and adding a device should produce a visible key change.','**Multiple devices**: devices need authenticated membership and protected sessions. Designs differ: Sesame supports per-user or per-device identity keys, and fan-out encrypts for relevant recipient devices. Adding a device need not change the account\'s identity fingerprint; device enrolment needs its own controls and visibility.')
e('give each member a sender chain key','give each sender a chain key')
e('so any member can update the group secret with O(log n) work. A 10,000-member group needs a path update touching about 14 nodes rather than 10,000 separate messages.','so a balanced populated tree can refresh a path of O(log n) nodes. A 10,000-member tree has roughly 14 ancestor levels. Actual update size/work also depends on blank nodes, resolution sizes and membership changes; the tree-depth example is not an unconditional total-cost bound.')
e('Signal\'s **sealed sender** hides the sender from the server for most messages, but the timing and recipient remain visible to it.','Signal\'s **sealed sender** reduces explicit sender identity exposed to the delivery service. Timing, recipient addressing and network observations can still reveal metadata; it is not guaranteed anonymity.')
e('X3DH (now PQXDH) uses uploaded prekeys and four DH computations','X3DH uses uploaded prekeys and three or four DH computations; PQXDH adds a post-quantum KEM')
e('so a stolen key cannot decrypt the past.','so current chain-state theft does not recover erased older keys; retained plaintext and skipped keys are separate exposures.')
e('so sessions heal after a compromise.','supporting recovery after attacker access ends and fresh unknown contributions arrive.')
e('safety numbers and key transparency stop it substituting its own.','authenticated safety-number comparison and verified transparency mechanisms help detect substitution under their respective assumptions.')
