f(3,'text','Cryptographic deniability: the shared-key tag alone is not transferable proof that Alice created the message, since Bob could compute it too',3)
f(3,'workedExample','Assuming authenticated, uncompromised pairwise keys, Bob can authenticate a received message because he knows he did not create its tag. A third party cannot distinguish which key holder computed that tag from the tag alone. This does not determine legal admissibility or eliminate external evidence; signatures also require trustworthy key attribution to establish a human author.')
f(4,'workedExample','DH1 and DH3 require Bob\'s signed-prekey private half; DH4 requires the optional one-time-prekey private half; DH2 additionally requires Bob\'s identity private half. Omitting DH2 lets an attacker with the used prekey private keys derive the secret without the identity private key. The server distributes public prekeys, not their private halves.')
f(5,'workedExample','With ck5 alone, an attacker derives mk5, ck6 and later keys in that chain, but cannot invert the KDF to recover erased earlier keys. A new chain derived from uncompromised root/DH state is outside this chain-key-only exposure. Theft of the whole device state is a stronger scenario and requires fresh unknown ratchet contributions before recovery.')
f(6,'explanation','Correct when the attacker loses secret-state access and enough fresh DH contributions unknown to them reach the peers. Recovery timing depends on compromise scope and message delivery.',1)
f(8,'workedExample','A stolen set of sender chain keys lets an attacker compute future keys in those chains; the one-way property protects the erased past, not the future. Recovery requires distributing fresh sender secrets through channels whose security has themselves recovered. Pairwise ratchets and MLS offer different recovery mechanisms, with guarantees conditional on fresh unknown contributions and delivered updates. MLS path length is logarithmic in a favourable populated tree, not a universal bound on all update costs.')
f(10,'prompt','Assume correct Signal E2EE with authenticated identities, uncompromised cryptographic algorithms and properly erased old message keys. Which threats can still expose communications? Select all that apply.')
f(10,'workedExample','Endpoint spyware can read plaintext; a recipient can retain it; sufficiently placed observers may correlate timing and traffic. A ciphertext-only relay cannot directly decrypt correctly protected content. Current ratchet-state theft does not recover erased past keys under the stated assumptions, but stored chat history, backups and retained skipped-message keys require separate analysis.')
f(11,'explanation','PQXDH does not make classical prekey authentication quantum-resistant. A sufficiently capable active quantum attacker would require resistant authentication; this is distinct from recorded-traffic confidentiality.',2)
